+\subsection{Existing results left as external contracts}
+The top-level algorithm theorems quantify over primitives satisfying the following properties. The Lean development does not construct efficient implementations satisfying these properties or prove the cited algorithmic theorems internally.
+
+\begin{table}[htbp]\centering\small
+\caption{External algorithmic results and their formal interfaces.}\label{tab:external}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}p{.25\linewidth}p{.70\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
+External result & Required property and interface \\ \midrule
+Fixed-dimensional ILP & A correct witness or infeasibility answer on the actual integer rows, and $r^{O(r)}\operatorname{poly}(B)$ cost: \lean{DenseRowSolverSpec}, \lean{DenseSolverCostBound}. See \cite{Lenstra,Kannan,Bredereck}. \\
+Exact rational rank & Correct rank of an integer matrix over $\mathbb Q$ and polynomial input cost: \lean{ExactIntegerRank}, \lean{BinaryRankOperations}, \lean{BinaryRankBounds}. The outer basis-extraction algorithm is proved here. \\
+Frank--Tardos & Small-sign preservation, the coefficient bound, and polynomial bit-time or strongly polynomial arithmetic-operation cost: \lean{CompressionPrimitive}, \lean{PreparationLocalBounds}, \lean{PreparationStrongBounds}. See \cite{FT,EKMR}. \\
+Elementary operations & Polynomial local costs of integer arithmetic, comparison, bounded access, grouping/sorting, set insertion, matrix emission, point construction, and relabeling. Interfaces include \lean{IntegerArithmeticCharge}, \lean{CountMatrixPreparation}, \lean{PointOperations}, \lean{NormalizationPrimitive}, and their local bounds. \\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+For these contracts the project proves the application-specific obligations: exact encodings, witness decoding, variable and row counts, integer operand bounds at reached states, parameter preservation, and composition of the costs of all phases. The existence and complexity of the external primitive implementations enter from the cited results or standard elementary algorithms. Thus a closed theorem asserting the existence of an efficient solver-independent Lean implementation is not part of the formalization. No efficient instance of these contracts is concealed as a project axiom; they remain visible hypotheses.
+
+Standard finite-dimensional linear algebra, convexity, finite-set reasoning, and integer arithmetic identities imported from Mathlib are already formalized results, not unformalized hypotheses. In particular, the project proves its rational-to-real rank bridge and the augmented-rank/affine-dimension relation using those library results. Conversely, the general complexity of an exact-rank algorithm is an external contract even though the algebraic definition of rank is formalized. The general rational-row denominator-clearing cost in Lemma~\ref{lem:oracle} is a standard arithmetic argument; the actual recognition path uses integer rows, whose construction and bounds are checked directly.
+
+The contextual DP-completeness and XP results cited in the introduction, historical novelty claims, and the six-item illustration in Section 6 are not certified by this Lean development. Nor does it prove compiler correctness, a machine operational cost model, or an end-to-end timing claim for Lean-generated code. These exclusions do not remove a structural lemma from Tables~\ref{tab:structural}--\ref{tab:algorithmic}; they specify the boundary of the verification claim.
 
